config SPL_MESON_USB_BOOT
	bool "SPL USB boot server (BROM take-over)"
	depends on MESON_GXL && SPL
	select SPL_RAM_DEVICE
	help
	  After DDR init, take the DWC2 over from the BootROM and serve the
	  Amlogic vendor protocol from SPL itself, so a host can push a FIT
	  into DRAM over the OTG cable at USB 2.0 high-speed bulk rates and
	  boot it -- no serial adapter and no removable media.

	  This is the bring-up and closed-loop development path.  Say N for a
	  production image that boots from SD/eMMC.

config MESON_G12A_XPL_CHAIN
	bool "G12A/SM1 TPL -> SPL chain (TPL in AHB SRAM, SPL in DRAM)"
	depends on MESON_G12A && TPL && SPL
	help
	  Split the open first stage like GXL libre (tpl-spl-chain-design).
	  TPL is the BootROM-loaded AHB stage under the hard 0xf000 amlimage
	  payload ceiling and owns DDR orchestration (Strategy A train-FW) and
	  the handoff block; SPL is relinked to run from DRAM and owns FIT /
	  LZ4/LZMA / SC load / ATF handoff.

	  Say N for the single-stage SRAM stub (L1 pass-1).

config MESON_G12A_SPL_ENTRY
	hex "DRAM address of the SPL stage"
	depends on MESON_G12A_XPL_CHAIN
	default 0x08000000
	help
	  Where TPL branches, and where the SPL stage is linked.  Must equal
	  SPL_TEXT_BASE.  Clears FIT staging 0x05000000 and BL31 0x05100000.

config MESON_G12A_SPL_OFFSET
	hex "Offset of the SPL stage inside the combined image"
	depends on MESON_G12A_XPL_CHAIN
	default 0x10000
	help
	  Where binman places the DRAM stage in u-boot-meson-with-spl.bin.
	  0x10000 is the end of the amlimage-signed SRAM stage (sm1/g12a
	  0x1000 header + 0xf000 payload) and the LBS_USB_BL2_SIZE split.

config MESON_G12A_SPL_MAX_SIZE
	hex "Bytes TPL reads for the SPL stage"
	depends on MESON_G12A_XPL_CHAIN
	default 0x30000
	help
	  How much TPL fetches from MESON_G12A_SPL_OFFSET.  Fixed window, not
	  a parsed header.  Default fills 0x10000..0x40000 (before FIT).

config MESON_G12A_FIT_OFFSET
	hex "Offset of the FIT inside the combined image"
	depends on MESON_G12A && SPL
	default SPL_PAD_TO
	help
	  Where binman places the FIT.  Chain fragment sets 0x40000 so the
	  DRAM SPL has room in the image; single-stage keeps SPL_PAD_TO.

config MESON_G12A_DRAM_MARGIN
	bool "TPL G12A/SM1 DRAM margin measurement (trained CSRs, shmoo, stress)"
	depends on MESON_G12A && TPL
	default n
	help
	  After DRAM bring-up, read the trained per-lane PHY state back out
	  (VrefDAC, TxDqDly, RxPBDly, RxEnDly, RxClkDly, DFIMRL, ATxDly),
	  shmoo VrefDAC / TxDqDly / RxClkDly away from the trained centre
	  until a data test fails, and stress-test with high-toggle
	  patterns over a large span.

	  This has to live in the TPL: measured on Solitude, the DDR PHY
	  APB window and the DMC BIST block read back all-zero from U-Boot
	  proper after BL31, and the TPL runs from SRAM so a deliberately
	  de-tuned DRAM cannot take the code with it.

	  Bring-up instrumentation, not a product option: it adds minutes
	  to boot and leaves patterns all over DRAM.  Default n.

config MESON_G12A_DRAM_TXDQS_CENTRE
	bool "TPL G12A/SM1: centre the write DQS (TxDqsDly) after training"
	depends on MESON_G12A && TPL
	default n
	help
	  After the vendor PMU training, search each DQ nibble's TxDqsDly
	  window with a CPU data probe (a lockstep sweep of the decoded
	  domain) and move the nibble to its window centre, shifting both
	  timing groups by the same delta.  The vendor values are put back
	  if the centred state probes bad.  Runs before the DRAM tests, so
	  they check the centred bus.

	  Measured on AML-S905D3-CC: the vendor-trained write DQS sits 4
	  steps from the late edge of an 18-step window on both ranks of
	  one unit, and this takes the worst side to 8; on a second,
	  single-rank part it takes the worst side from 6/8 to 9.  It
	  writes test patterns into 512 KiB at 0x30000000.  Default n until
	  it is soaked on the production image.

	  It is entered two ways (the quick and slow bring-up): a quick
	  check after training (see ..._THRESH) and, if the DRAM test then
	  finds a bad word and the centring has not run, as a fallback
	  before the test is run again.

config MESON_G12A_DRAM_TXDQS_CENTRE_THRESH
	int "Quick check: centre only if the write DQS fails within +-N steps"
	depends on MESON_G12A_DRAM_TXDQS_CENTRE
	default 6
	help
	  After training, every nibble's TxDqsDly is moved by +N and -N and
	  the data probe run at each; the full centring runs only if either
	  fails, i.e. when the worst-side write margin is below N steps.  0
	  centres on every boot.  Measured late edges: +5 on both ranks of
	  one AML-S905D3-CC unit (centres at 6), +7 / +9 on a single-rank
	  part (does not).

config MESON_G12A_DRAM_TXDQS_CENTRE_SPAN
	int "Centring: sweep only the trained values +-N decoded codes"
	depends on MESON_G12A_DRAM_TXDQS_CENTRE
	default 24
	help
	  The centring's window search visits the decoded TxDqsDly codes
	  from (lowest trained value - N) to (highest trained value + N)
	  instead of all 256.  If any nibble's best window touches that
	  range's edge, or a nibble finds no window, it sweeps all 256
	  before applying anything ("TXS <lo> <hi> full").  0 always sweeps
	  all 256.  Measured windows: 17-21 steps, the trained point 4-13
	  steps from each edge, on two parts.

config MESON_G12A_DRAM_OPEN
	bool "TPL G12A/SM1 open host-side DDR init (transcribed a644 steps)"
	depends on MESON_G12A && TPL
	default n
	help
	  Perform the host side of the vendor DDR driver_init (a644)
	  ourselves, from the instruction-by-instruction transcriptions of
	  the timing engine, the DMC/PCTL program, the PHY static config,
	  the PMU message block, both pin-mux tables, the IMEM/DMEM loader,
	  the PIE image load and the DMC bring-up -- and call the vendor
	  PMU firmware only for training, over the UCT mailbox.

	  Two arms, selected in dram-g12a.c: G12A_OPEN_COMPARE (default)
	  leaves the blob's own walk in place and judges every open
	  computation read-only against the state it left; G12A_OPEN_LIVE
	  replaces the walk.

	  The PMU firmware blob stays either way -- it is what measures the
	  eye.  Two host-side steps are still the blob because they are not
	  transcribed: S1 (fffb931c, PLL / DMC address map + geometry) and
	  T10 (fffb8af0, the DMC<->PHY handshake state machine).

	  Bring-up instrumentation, not a product option.  Default n.

config MESON_G12A_DRAM_OPEN_LIVE
	bool "TPL G12A/SM1 open DDR init: RUN it instead of comparing"
	depends on MESON_G12A_DRAM_OPEN
	default n
	help
	  Select the LIVE arm: the open path performs a644's S2..T14 itself
	  and the blob is called only for S1 and T10.  Without this, the
	  option builds the compare-only arm, which is read-only and cannot
	  perturb the boot.  Default n.

config MESON_G12A_DRAM_PROBE
	bool "TPL G12A/SM1 open DDR: black-box training probe + cfg overlay"
	depends on MESON_G12A_DRAM_OPEN
	default n
	help
	  Debug-only arms for black-box DRAM bring-up on a part the vendor
	  12-cfg table does not know (qi:N1294). Three effects, all inert
	  when this is n:

	  (b) replace the step-9 1D-TRAIN blob call (0xFFFB8D74, whose
	      mailbox poll has no terminal exit and dead-stops on an
	      off-table part) with the open UCT mailbox transcribed with a
	      hard message/wall bound; on every non-OK terminal print a
	      bounded register dump (DMC window, UCT mailbox state, micro
	      reset/mux, per-lane harvested vref banks, msg-block head) so
	      each boot returns a SIGNAL instead of a silent hang.

	  (a) after the built-in ddr_set_t is built, overlay a cfg block
	      read from a fixed OCRAM address the host uploads each run, so
	      a geometry sweep is a re-upload, not a rebuild.

	  (c) a MR/geometry dump of the fields the driver actually applies.

	  Ships DEFAULT n: no shipped board config selects it. Default n.

config MESON_G12A_PPT_DDC
	bool "TPL G12A/SM1 opt-in LPDDR4 PPT/DDC after 1D/2D"
	depends on MESON_G12A && TPL
	default n
	help
	  After 1D/2D + T7 (which zeros PPTTrainSetup) and T10 (DMC DFI
	  PHY-Master ack already HW), program PPTTrainSetup PS0/PS1 with
	  the Synopsys phyinit default packing and restore PptCtlStatic
	  from the T8 ddr_set_t snapshot (ds+250).

	  Encoding is PUB 2.20a §11.3.227 Table 11-235, not invented:
	  PhyMstrTrainInterval[3:0] 0xA = 268435456 MEMCLKs, and
	  PhyMstrMaxReqToAck[6:4] 0x5 = 8192 MEMCLKs; either 0 disables
	  the PHY Master (DDC off).  Packed value 0x005A
	  (G12A_DDR_PHY_PPTTRAINSETUP_ST_DDC).  Vendor aml_ddr.fw T7
	  writes 0 — enabling DDC is opt-in, not copy-from-closed.
	  No software dfi_phymstr_ack loop (DMC HW, G12-C2).

	  Default n keeps residual-opensc at interval 0.  Enable via
	  configs/tpl-g12a-ppt-ddc.config for a bring-up boot only.

config MESON_G12A_DRAM_WIDTH
	bool "TPL G12A/SM1 offline eye-width crumb at trained centre"
	depends on MESON_G12A && TPL
	default n
	help
	  After 1D/2D, reuse the existing DRAM-margin walker to print
	  eye WIDTH around the trained centre — SoC RX VrefDAC0/1
	  (csr 0x10040, VrefDAC0 DBYTE0 lane0) and write DQS
	  TxDqsDlyTg0 (csr 0x100D0).  Each arm stops at the first
	  failing probe; the bound is G12A_MRG_SWEEP_MAX.  No soak,
	  no extremes, no DDC, not a product option.

	  Default n.  Set in the aml-s905d3-cc_libre_*_width defconfigs
	  for a bring-up residual boot only.

	  The BL33 opt-in command (CMD_MESON_G12A_DRAM, `g12a_dram`)
	  dumps this centre / reuses this walker from the U-Boot
	  prompt.  It is not this TPL crumb and is not run from
	  board_init/PREBOOT.

config MESON_G12B_POWER_INIT
	bool "G12B Alta dual-cluster power init (VCCK_A/B + VDDEE)"
	depends on MESON_G12A && TPL
	default n
	help
	  Compile spl-g12b.o, whose strong meson_power_init() raises
	  VCCK_A (GPIOAO_4), VCCK_B/VCC3V3 (TEST_N AO_GPIO_O[31] +
	  AO_SEC_REG0[0]) and VDDEE (PWM_AO_B) for AML-A311D-CC.

	  This is the board power half only, and it is deliberately
	  INDEPENDENT of MESON_G12_OPEN_PLL: the Strategy A orchestrator
	  in dram-g12a.o supplies dram_init() while this supplies the
	  G12B power sequence, which is what the SM1 TPL->SPL chain needs
	  in order to run on Alta.  When it is set, dram-g12a.c's own
	  meson_power_init() (Solitude PWM tables) is compiled out.

config MESON_G12_OPEN_PLL
	bool "G12B Alta Strategy B Phase 1: open DDR PLL lock (no train)"
	depends on MESON_G12A && TPL
	select MESON_G12B_POWER_INIT
	default n
	help
	  Compile dram-g12.o instead of Strategy A dram-g12a.o, on top of
	  the MESON_G12B_POWER_INIT power sequence it selects: DDR PLL
	  lock poll on AM_DDR_PLL_CNTL0 bit 31, then hang.
	  Does not load PHY FW, does not train, does not enable DDC.
	  Mutually exclusive with the SM1 residual-opensc orchestrator
	  (that tree keeps dram-g12a.o when this is n).

config TPL_MESON_G12A_USB_RESIDUAL
	bool "TPL G12A/SM1 USB residual (re-enum + bulk SPL)"
	depends on MESON_G12A && TPL && MESON_G12A_XPL_CHAIN
	default n
	help
	  After train stubs, soft-disconnect the BROM USB link and soft-
	  connect a minimal DWC2 worldcup server so the host can push DRAM
	  SPL to 0x08000000 (and optional train holds) over USB.  G12 bases
	  only (0xFF400000 DWC2 / 0xFFE09000 glue) — never GXL 0xc910/0xd007.

	  Option 2 of open-tpl-usb-residual.md.  Default n keeps pass-1
	  size/behaviour unchanged until residual is measured on Solitude.
	  Set in the aml-s905d3-cc_libre_* defconfigs, which are the
	  residual-HW bring-up targets.
	  UART crumbs: U attach, u fail, R receive start.  Must stay under
	  TPL 0xf000 payload ceiling.

config TPL_MESON_G12A_USB_W1_HOLD
	bool "TPL G12A/SM1 USB residual: NAK EP2 after window 1's first frame"
	depends on TPL_MESON_G12A_USB_RESIDUAL
	default n
	help
	  After the first complete G12R frame in the pre-dram_init window,
	  stop re-arming EP2 until the post-dram_init window opens, so a
	  frame the host queues right behind it (the SPL+FIT frame) waits on
	  the wire instead of being parsed before DRAM exists.  Only for a
	  host that sends the residual as ONE frame (the --legacy-tail-cut
	  concat); a host that sends aml/piei/1d/2d as separate frames in
	  window 1 must leave this n.  Plan: tpl-usb-readiness-plan.md.

config TPL_MESON_G12A_EMMC_REG
	bool "TPL G12A/SM1 register-level eMMC reader (READ ONLY)"
	depends on MESON_G12A && TPL && MESON_G12A_XPL_CHAIN
	default n
	help
	  A read-only eMMC reader for the SRAM stage with no driver model,
	  no OF_CONTROL and no TPL DTB: it drives SD_EMMC_C (0xFFE07000)
	  through its registers and moves each 512 B block through the
	  controller's own buffer at base+0x400 by CPU, the path the SM1
	  BootROM itself uses (FUN_ffff4a6c).  The driver-model reader does
	  not fit beside the shipping TPL (.sram over by 8208 B).

	  It re-initialises the card (CMD0, CMD1, CMD2, CMD3, CMD7) in 1-bit
	  mode at the BootROM's own clock words, and keeps the BootROM's pin
	  mux, pulls and clock gate as they are.  Reading a boot area
	  selects it with CMD6 in set-bits / clear-bits mode on
	  PARTITION_CONFIG bits [2:0] (PARTITION_ACCESS) only: that field is
	  volatile (cleared by CMD0 and power loss), and BOOT_PARTITION_ENABLE
	  is never written.  No write command is compiled in.

	  With this option the TPL runs a self-test after the second USB
	  window: crc32 of user sector 0, of user LBA 1..128 (the 64 KiB the
	  BootROM reads) and of boot0 sector 0, and the elapsed time.  Never
	  fatal.  Plan: amlogic/software/boot/g12/plan/sm1-resident-chain.md.

config TPL_MESON_G12A_EMMC_FETCH
	bool "TPL G12A/SM1: fetch both residual windows from eMMC, not USB"
	depends on TPL_MESON_G12A_EMMC_REG && TPL_MESON_G12A_USB_RESIDUAL
	default n
	help
	  Replace the two USB residual windows with reads from a staged
	  layout on the eMMC, through the register-level reader.  Window 1
	  (the DDR residual concat) is read block by block into the SAME
	  splitter the USB path feeds (bulk_copy_pay() in meson-usb-g12.c),
	  so the SRAM holds and has_2d come out as a USB delivery leaves
	  them.  Window 2 (the DRAM stage and the boot FIT, the binman image
	  from SPL_OFFSET on) is read after dram_init(): [0, 0x30000) to
	  0x08000000, the rest to 0x02000000, and its length is reported to
	  the handoff as the USB path reports it.

	  The layout is one header block at TPL_MESON_G12A_EMMC_FETCH_LBA of
	  hardware partition TPL_MESON_G12A_EMMC_FETCH_PART, written by
	  amlogic/software/tools/mk-g12a-emmc-stage.py: magic, the LBA,
	  length and crc32 of each window, and a crc32 of the header.  The
	  TPL takes offsets and lengths only from that header and checks
	  every crc, so the TPL constants and the staged file cannot drift.

	  The source is selected by this option, not by the BootROM's boot
	  device: the TPL itself still arrives over USB.  The USB windows are
	  not served when it is set.  Read only, as the reader is.

config TPL_MESON_G12A_EMMC_FETCH_PART
	int "eMMC hardware partition holding the staged layout (1 = boot0)"
	depends on TPL_MESON_G12A_EMMC_FETCH
	range 0 2
	default 1

config TPL_MESON_G12A_EMMC_FETCH_LBA
	hex "LBA of the staged layout header in that partition"
	depends on TPL_MESON_G12A_EMMC_FETCH
	default 0x800
	help
	  0x800 = 1 MiB into the partition, far from LBA 1..128, the only
	  window the SM1 BootROM reads, so a staged layout leaves the
	  BootROM's view of the medium unchanged.

config MESON_G12A_RESIDENT
	bool "G12A/SM1 resident chain: boot-device dispatch, FIT from the TPL, USB fallback"
	depends on MESON_G12A_XPL_CHAIN && TPL_MESON_G12A_EMMC_FETCH
	default n
	help
	  The chain for a TPL the BootROM loads from eMMC (LBA 1 of a
	  hardware partition) rather than over USB.  One symbol for all
	  three phases:

	  TPL: the residual windows come from the staged eMMC layout when
	  the BootROM's boot device is eMMC and from USB otherwise (both
	  paths are linked).  On any boot device but USB the TPL's first act
	  arms the USB fallback: AO_RTI_STATUS_REG2 = 0x4f5244b1, which
	  makes the BootROM enter USB before any storage and wait for the
	  host with no timeout on the next reset, and the EE watchdog, which
	  supplies that reset if anything up to U-Boot proper hangs.  While
	  armed, the TPL pets the watchdog where it used to stop it (USB
	  window, eMMC window 1, DRAM PLL init, training).  A power cycle
	  clears the word.

	  SPL (DRAM stage): when the handoff says the TPL delivered the
	  boot FIT to DRAM, spl_boot_device() answers BOOT_DEVICE_RAM on any
	  boot device, instead of raw MMC sector reads.

	  U-Boot proper: at EVT_DM_POST_INIT_R, if the word is armed or the
	  TPL armed the EE watchdog (MESON_G12A_RESIDENT_OVERRIDE=n arms it
	  without the word), the
	  driver-model watchdog takes the EE watchdog over (petted every
	  second while U-Boot runs) and the word stays armed; at the last
	  step before the OS (board_quiesce_devices(), booti/bootm and
	  UEFI) it is cleared through SiP SET_USB_BOOT_FUNC (0x82000043,
	  CLEAR) -- a non-secure write to it faults.  A reset or panic in
	  U-Boot before that goes to the rig; `usbfb clear` clears it by
	  hand.  Needs a TF-A built with AML_USB_FALLBACK=1, which answers
	  that call with the register write; a TF-A without it forwards the
	  call to an SCP over SCPI.

	  Plan: amlogic/software/boot/g12/plan/sm1-resident-chain.md
	  "USB fallback" and "Recovery gaps".

config MESON_G12A_RESIDENT_WDT_MS
	int "EE watchdog timeout the resident TPL arms (ms)"
	depends on MESON_G12A_RESIDENT
	range 1000 65535
	default 60000
	help
	  1 ms ticks, so 65535 is the register's ceiling.  One window from
	  the TPL's first act to U-Boot proper's EVT_DM_POST_INIT_R, petted
	  only by the TPL's own loops (USB window, training); U-Boot proper
	  then restarts it with this timeout and pets it.

config MESON_G12A_RESIDENT_OVERRIDE
	bool "Resident chain: arm the BootROM USB-override word with the watchdog"
	depends on MESON_G12A_RESIDENT
	default y
	help
	  y (the "rig" mode): the TPL writes AO_RTI_STATUS_REG2 = 0x4f5244b1
	  beside the EE watchdog, so a hang up to the OS resets into an
	  endless BootROM USB wait -- right for a board with a USB-BootROM
	  host attached.

	  n (the "standalone" mode): the TPL arms the EE watchdog alone, so a
	  hang resets and runs the normal boot again -- for a board with no
	  USB host, where an endless USB wait would strand it.  U-Boot proper
	  then takes the watchdog over on the TPL's own arm (EN set and the
	  timeout equal to MESON_G12A_RESIDENT_WDT_MS), not on the word.

	  Plan: amlogic/software/boot/g12/plan/sm1-two-stage-watchdog.md 1a.

config MESON_G12A_RESIDENT_ARM_ON_USB
	bool "Resident chain: TEST ONLY -- arm the watchdog on a USB boot too"
	depends on MESON_G12A_RESIDENT
	default n
	help
	  A USB-delivered (DRAM-boot) TPL normally does not arm, because the
	  USB path is already where the fallback leads.  This makes it arm
	  anyway, so the watchdog stages can be gated from a USB-delivered
	  image without writing boot media.  Never set in a shipping
	  defconfig.

config MESON_G12A_RESIDENT_M20_PROBE
	bool "Resident chain: TEST ONLY -- TPL samples the SC and both watchdogs"
	depends on MESON_G12A_RESIDENT
	default n
	help
	  The TPL prints the EE watchdog as the reset left it, then two
	  samples 0.5 s apart of the SC heartbeat tick, the SC's AO-watchdog
	  pet count and the AO watchdog CNTL, before any SC is loaded on
	  this boot.  Answers whether the SC survives an EE watchdog reset
	  (plan sm1-two-stage-watchdog.md M2.0).  Never set in a shipping
	  defconfig.

config MESON_G12A_RESIDENT_M21_AO_SPIN
	bool "Resident chain: TEST ONLY -- TPL stops the EE watchdog and spins"
	depends on MESON_G12A_RESIDENT
	default n
	help
	  As its first act the TPL prints the AO and EE watchdog CNTL, clears
	  the EE watchdog's enable (the BootROM's own arm included), and spins
	  forever, with no SC loaded on this boot.  Only the AO watchdog can
	  then reset the chip: answers whether it backstops a wedge before the
	  SC loads (plan sm1-two-stage-watchdog.md M2.1).  The board never
	  boots with it set.  Never set in a shipping defconfig.

config TPL_MESON_USB_REENUM
	bool "TPL re-enumerates as its own USB device, chip id as serial"
	depends on TPL_MESON_USB_BOOT || TPL_MESON_G12A_USB_RESIDUAL
	default n
	help
	  Instead of continuing the BootROM's USB session, the TPL drops off
	  the bus for 10 ms after DRAM init and comes back as its own device,
	  whose iSerialNumber is the chip id (include/chip_serial.h) -- so a
	  host sees the board's identity from the TPL on.  The host must follow
	  the device by its port (tools/meson-usb-boot.py and the G12 harness
	  do); the re-enumeration costs about 0.5 s.

	  Only for the USB flashing (DFU) targets the Libre Computer Android
	  app uses (*_libre_dfu_defconfig).  Product targets keep the BootROM
	  session.
	  GXL: PHY power-on reset restart with U-Boot's GUSBCFG and the
	  BootROM's FIFO map.  G12: a soft disconnect before the take-over,
	  keeping the BootROM's configuration.

config SPL_MESON_USBFLASH_WRITE
	bool "SPL USB flash session writes MMC storage"
	depends on SPL_USBFLASH && SPL_MMC_WRITE
	select SPL_USBFLASH_WRITE
	default n
	help
	  The SPL flash session (common/spl/spl_usbflash.c) is
	  read-only by default.  With this, its eMMC/SD alternates take DFU
	  downloads and `usbflash ums mmc <n>` serves a read-write LUN.  SPI
	  NOR stays read-only.  Only for the USB flashing (DFU) targets
	  (*_libre_dfu_defconfig); product targets keep the read-only session.

config TPL_MESON_USB_BOOT
	bool "TPL USB boot server (BROM take-over)"
	depends on MESON_GXL && TPL
	help
	  The same DWC2 device server, built into the TPL stage instead.

	  In the TPL->SPL->FIT chain the SRAM stage owns DDR init AND the
	  receive path: it takes the DWC2 over from the BootROM, pulls the
	  whole session into DRAM, fills the handoff block and only then tears
	  the device down and jumps.  Carrying a live enumerated link across a
	  stage boundary is the one thing this stack must not do, so SPL never
	  touches USB and there is no "SPL takes over the link" option here.

	  Enable this together with SPL_MESON_USB_BOOT only while the single
	  stage SPL is still the shipping path; they build the same sources
	  into two independent binaries.

config MESON_GX_XPL_CHAIN
	bool "GX TPL -> SPL chain (TPL in SRAM, SPL in DRAM)"
	depends on MESON_GXL && TPL && SPL
	help
	  Split the loader in two.  TPL is the BootROM-loaded SRAM stage under
	  the hard 0xb000 amlimage payload ceiling and owns DDR init, the boot
	  source and the handoff block; SPL is relinked to run from DRAM, where
	  no comparable ceiling exists, and owns the FIT and the decompressors.

	  This is a global symbol on purpose: it is read in BOTH phases, because
	  it is what tells the SPL phase that it is the DRAM stage and must not
	  re-run any of the SRAM stage's work.

	  Say N for the single-stage SPL, which is still the shipping path and
	  is built byte-identically when this is off.

config MESON_GX_SPL_ENTRY
	hex "DRAM address of the SPL stage"
	depends on MESON_GX_XPL_CHAIN
	default 0x08000000
	help
	  Where TPL branches, and where the SPL stage is linked.  It must equal
	  SPL_TEXT_BASE -- the DRAM stage checks the two against each other via
	  the handoff block rather than hanging if they ever drift apart.

	  The default clears every address the FIT path uses, on both layouts:

	    vendor           BL33 0x01000000, SCP 0x013c0000, BL31 0x05100000
	    LIBRE_SECURE     BL33 0x04000000, SCP 0x00001000, BL31 0x00100000

	  FIT staging is not a property of the layout -- SPL_LOAD_FIT_ADDRESS is
	  set per board: 0x02000000 on libretech-cc_libre_defconfig, 0x0c000000
	  on libretech-cc_v2_libre_defconfig and libretech-ac_libre_defconfig.
	  0x08000000 sits between them and clears both.

	  Distinct again from MESON_SPL_FIT_BUFFER_ADDR 0x05000000 (spl.c), the
	  FIT WORKING buffer under MESON_GX_LIBRE_SECURE_LAYOUT, which no board
	  uses as a staging address.

config TPL_MESON_GX_BOOTLOG
	bool "GX chain: keep the TPL's UART output in the boot log"
	depends on MESON_GX_XPL_CHAIN && TPL_SERIAL
	default y
	help
	  Copy every byte the TPL writes to the AO UART into the boot log
	  (arch-meson/gx-bootlog.h): in SRAM until DDR is up, then at
	  MESON_GX_BOOTLOG_ADDR in DRAM.  A host can read it over the TPL's USB
	  protocol, and the DRAM stage replays it on its USB console.

config SPL_MESON_GX_BOOTLOG
	bool "GX chain: keep the SPL's UART output and replay the boot log on USB"
	depends on MESON_GX_XPL_CHAIN && SPL_SERIAL
	default y
	help
	  The DRAM stage appends its own UART output to the boot log the TPL
	  started, and its USB flash session replays the whole log into the
	  CDC ACM console when that console starts (SPL_USB_GADGET_DNL_ACM), so
	  a host with no serial cable sees the boot from the TPL's first line.

config MESON_GX_BOOTLOG_ADDR
	hex "DRAM address of the GX boot log"
	depends on TPL_MESON_GX_BOOTLOG || SPL_MESON_GX_BOOTLOG
	default 0x07f00000
	help
	  Header plus data, MESON_GX_BOOTLOG_SIZE bytes.  The default sits below
	  the DRAM stage (MESON_GX_SPL_ENTRY 0x08000000) and above every other
	  address the chain writes before U-Boot proper: FIT staging 0x02000000
	  and 0x0c000000, BL33 0x04000000, the FIT working buffer 0x05000000
	  (1 MiB), the SPL stack below 0x09000000 and its heap at 0x0a000000.

config MESON_GX_BOOTLOG_SIZE
	hex "Size of the GX boot log in DRAM, header included"
	depends on TPL_MESON_GX_BOOTLOG || SPL_MESON_GX_BOOTLOG
	default 0x10000

config MESON_GX_SPL_OFFSET
	hex "Offset of the SPL stage inside the combined image"
	depends on MESON_GX_XPL_CHAIN
	default 0xc000
	help
	  Where binman places the DRAM stage in u-boot-meson-with-spl.bin, and
	  therefore where TPL looks for it on every medium.

	  0xc000 is the end of the amlimage-signed SRAM stage and the start of
	  the padding that runs to the FIT (MESON_GX_FIT_OFFSET: 0x1fe00 in the
	  original layout, 0x4c000 on the libretech -libre boards since the
	  window was widened for the SPL flash session).  It is also the LBS split point
	  (build.sh: dd bs=49152), so the second protocol file begins exactly
	  with SPL.

	  Must be a multiple of 512: the MMC loader reads whole sectors.

config MESON_GX_SPL_MAX_SIZE
	hex "Bytes TPL reads for the SPL stage"
	depends on MESON_GX_XPL_CHAIN
	default 0x13e00
	help
	  How much TPL fetches from MESON_GX_SPL_OFFSET.  It is a fixed length,
	  not a parsed one: the raw stage carries no header, so TPL reads a
	  window and says in the handoff block exactly how many bytes it read
	  rather than claiming to know the image's true size.

	  The default is the whole gap of the original layout (0xc000..0x1fe00).
	  A board that moves the FIT sets this to MESON_GX_FIT_OFFSET -
	  MESON_GX_SPL_OFFSET (the libretech -libre boards: 0x40000, FIT at
	  0x4c000); gx-handoff.h asserts it covers SPL_SIZE_LIMIT and stops at the
	  FIT.  Must be a multiple of 512.

config TPL_MESON_GX_LOAD_MMC
	bool "TPL fetches SPL from SD/eMMC"
	depends on MESON_GX_XPL_CHAIN && TPL_MMC && TPL_BLK && TPL_DM_MMC
	help
	  Teach the SRAM stage to read the DRAM stage off the medium it booted
	  from, when that medium is an SD card or eMMC.

	  This is a raw block read of MESON_GX_SPL_MAX_SIZE bytes from
	  MESON_GX_SPL_OFFSET inside the combined image -- not a call into
	  common/spl/spl_mmc.c.  That file is written around an image FORMAT
	  (it parses a FIT or a legacy header and fills a struct spl_image_info)
	  and around the generic board_init_r() flow, and the DRAM stage is a
	  raw blob loaded by a stage that never returns from board_init_f().
	  Reusing blk_dread() and blk_dselect_hwpart() gets the block layer
	  without inheriting either assumption.

	  Says nothing about USB, which is a push and needs no loader, nor
	  about SPI NOR, which is its own arm and its own Kconfig symbol.

	  Needs no CLK uclass, and deliberately does not enable one.  The probe
	  used to die on clk_get_bulk()'s -ENOSYS stub; meson_mmc_probe() now
	  treats that as non-fatal in the TPL phase, because the BootROM has
	  already driven this controller to fetch TPL itself and the only
	  register that has to move afterwards, SD_EMMC_CLOCK, is programmed by
	  a plain meson_write() (measured on AML-S905X-CC 2026-08-07: the HHI
	  gates read on, SD_EMMC_CLOCK read 0, and writing it directly took
	  SD_EMMC_CFG from a bus fault to 0x00004890).  Turning TPL_CLK on
	  instead would cost at least 23864 bytes of .sram overflow.

	  BUILD-VERIFIED ONLY: no board has run this.  A successful probe is a
	  runtime property, and pin muxing is likewise inherited from the ROM
	  (there is no TPL_PINCTRL either).  A failure still reports a
	  GX_TPL_LOAD_* code rather than reading garbage.

config MESON_GX_MMC_IMAGE_OFFSET
	hex "Byte offset of the combined image in the MMC user area"
	depends on TPL_MESON_GX_LOAD_MMC
	default 0x200
	help
	  Where u-boot-meson-with-spl.bin starts on an SD card or in the eMMC
	  user area.  512 is the Amlogic convention -- sector 0 is left to the
	  partition table -- and it is the value that makes the existing
	  SYS_MMCSD_RAW_MODE_U_BOOT_SECTOR of 0x100 agree with the FIT at
	  0x1fe00 in the image: 0x1fe00 + 0x200 = 0x20000 = sector 0x100.

config MESON_GX_MMC_BOOT_IMAGE_OFFSET
	hex "Byte offset of the combined image in an eMMC boot partition"
	depends on TPL_MESON_GX_LOAD_MMC
	default 0x0
	help
	  Same, for boot0/boot1.  A hardware boot partition has no partition
	  table to step over, so the image starts at zero.

config MESON_GX_MMC_HWPART
	int "eMMC hardware partition holding SPL (-1 = ask the card)"
	depends on TPL_MESON_GX_LOAD_MMC
	default -1
	range -1 2
	help
	  0 = user area, 1 = boot0, 2 = boot1.

	  -1 reads EXT_CSD_PART_CONF's BOOT_PARTITION_ENABLE, which is the
	  card's own record of where the ROM was told to boot from, and uses
	  that: 1 -> boot0, 2 -> boot1, anything else -> the user area.  Pin it
	  only for a board whose flashing layout disagrees with its eMMC.

	  SD cards have no hardware partitions and ignore this entirely.

config MESON_GX_DMC_DUMP
	bool "Print the programmed DMC geometry after meson_dram_dmc_init()"
	depends on MESON_GX
	help
	  Print DMC_DDR_CTRL and DDR0_ADDRMAP_0..4 as READ BACK off the
	  controller, at the end of meson_dram_dmc_init() -- i.e. after
	  meson_dram_dmc_set_addrmap() has written them and BEFORE the first
	  DRAM access.  One printf, no register writes, nothing else.

	  READ ONLY, and by construction: the only statement it adds is a
	  printf() of six readl()s.

	  The printf itself already existed, guarded on the DRAM alias test and
	  the DRAM fault classifier -- neither of which is a Kconfig symbol on
	  this branch, so the block was dead code here and the only way to read
	  a programmed ADDRMAP word back was to build an instrument branch that
	  carries a DIFFERENT map.  That is exactly the wrong instrument for
	  the question "did the map this branch programs land?".

	  Same argument, and same answer, as TPL_MESON_GX_NOR_PROBE and
	  TPL_MESON_GX_MMC_PROBE: it runs inside board_init_f, so in the
	  TPL->SPL chain it lands in PASS 1, on a rig that cannot complete a
	  pass-2 FIT transfer at all.  Say Y on a bring-up rig; it costs code
	  in a stage with a hard ceiling and buys a diagnostic.

config TPL_MESON_GX_NOR_PROBE
	bool "TPL reads the SPI NOR JEDEC ID and one block (READ ONLY)"
	depends on MESON_GX_XPL_CHAIN && TPL_DM_SPI && MESON_SPIFC
	help
	  Issue two commands to the onboard SPI NOR from the SRAM stage --
	  0x9F (read identification) and 0x03 (read data) -- and print what
	  comes back.  Nothing else.

	  It exists because a clock fix is worth exactly what a transaction
	  proves.  meson_spifc could not get a rate in a phase without the CLK
	  uclass; spifc-clk-gx.c now reads clk81 out of the HIU registers
	  instead, and the cheapest end-to-end check of pinmux, controller,
	  divider, chip select and both data directions is a JEDEC ID.

	  READ ONLY, and by construction rather than by intent: this path binds
	  a generic SPI slave, not the spi-nor core, so no erase or write
	  callback and no WREN opcode is compiled into the stage at all.  Say Y
	  on a bring-up rig; it costs code in a stage with a hard ceiling and
	  buys a diagnostic, not a boot source.

	  Runs on every boot device, including USB -- otherwise a board that
	  boots over the cable never exercises the path being tested.

config MESON_GX_MMC_SD_DEVNUM
	int "u-boot MMC device number of the SD card slot"
	depends on TPL_MESON_GX_LOAD_MMC
	default 1
	help
	  Which /aliases mmcN the SD card slot is, so gx_tpl_fetch_spl() can
	  turn BOOT_DEVICE_SD into a device number.

	  ONE, not zero, and this is a correction rather than a preference.
	  meson-gx.dtsi annotates `mmc0 = &sd_emmc_c` as "SD card", but both
	  AML-S905X-CC and AML-S905X-CC-V2 put the SD card on sd_emmc_b and
	  eMMC on sd_emmc_c -- their .dts files label the two nodes in exactly
	  those words, and the SD node is the one carrying cd-gpios and the
	  sd-uhs-* modes.  So on our GXL boards mmc0 is eMMC and mmc1 is SD,
	  the opposite of what the SoC dtsi's comment says.

	  The dispatch used to spell this `bd == BOOT_DEVICE_EMMC ? 1 : 0`,
	  which is inverted on both boards.  It could not be observed before
	  2026-08-30 because the mmc nodes carried no bootph-* mark, so no
	  controller ever bound and the wrong devnum was never reached.

config MESON_GX_MMC_EMMC_DEVNUM
	int "u-boot MMC device number of the eMMC"
	depends on TPL_MESON_GX_LOAD_MMC
	default 0
	help
	  Which /aliases mmcN the eMMC is.  Zero on both GXL boards -- see
	  MESON_GX_MMC_SD_DEVNUM for why that is not a typo.

config TPL_MESON_GX_MMC_PROBE
	bool "TPL binds SD/eMMC and reads sector 0 (READ ONLY)"
	depends on MESON_GX_XPL_CHAIN && TPL_MMC && TPL_BLK && TPL_DM_MMC
	help
	  Bind both boot-capable MMC controllers from the SRAM stage, initialise
	  whatever card answers, and print the block size, capacity, EXT_CSD
	  boot-partition config and the first 16 bytes of sector 0.  Nothing
	  else.

	  READ ONLY, and by construction rather than by intent: the only block
	  entry point compiled in is blk_dread().  No blk_dwrite, no erase, no
	  mmc_switch of a write-protect or boot-config field.

	  It exists because TPL_MESON_GX_LOAD_MMC cannot be exercised on the
	  bring-up rig at all.  The loader is reached only from
	  gx_tpl_fetch_spl()'s BOOT_DEVICE_SD/EMMC cases, and
	  meson_get_boot_device() reports what the BootROM actually booted
	  from -- BOOT_DEVICE_USB on every run the USB dev loop can perform.
	  So the loader is dead code until an image is flashed to a card, and
	  flashing a card is the step nobody should take before the path has
	  read one byte.  Same argument, and same answer, as
	  TPL_MESON_GX_NOR_PROBE: runs on every boot device, including USB.

	  Say Y on a bring-up rig.  It costs code in a stage with a hard
	  ceiling and buys a diagnostic, not a boot source.

config MESON_GX_FIT_OFFSET
	hex "Offset of the FIT inside the combined image"
	depends on MESON_GX && SPL
	default SPL_PAD_TO
	help
	  Where binman places the FIT.  This used to be spelled CONFIG_SPL_PAD_TO
	  directly, which conflated two unrelated things: SPL_PAD_TO is a linker
	  pad for the SRAM stage and include/config_fallbacks.h forbids it from
	  dropping below SPL_MAX_SIZE, while this is a position in an on-media
	  image.  Defaulting to SPL_PAD_TO keeps every existing board where it is.

config MESON_GXL_SCP_BOARD_ID
	hex "Board id of the open SC payload this build pairs with"
	depends on MESON_GXL
	default 0
	help
	  The G5/D3 pairing contract (N54 stage). The open SC firmware is
	  built ONE BOARD AT A TIME (scp-firmware GXL_BOARD=<slug>, ledger
	  N339/G5): its .bin carries the board id as its LAST word --
	  0xB0A4 << 16 | contract-version << 8 | id, never the first, the M3
	  vector table lives there -- and the boot FIT's scp image node carries
	  the same id as `board-id` (binman feeds it from this symbol).
	  arch/arm/mach-meson/spl-gxl-scp.c reads the staged payload's last
	  word BEFORE the SC-ROM 0xd3 fetch and refuses a mismatch
	  ("SCP: board mismatch, refusing fetch"), degrading to a boot without
	  the SC -- because in a per-board-binary world a mispaired slice is
	  otherwise SILENT: the ROM never verifies BL30 and the FIT's SHA-256
	  covers corruption, not the wrong board.

	  The numbers are the registry in scp-firmware
	  product/amlogic/gxl/include/gxl_board.h: 1 aml-s905x-cc,
	  2 aml-s905x-cc-v2, 3 aml-s805x-ac, 4 aml-s905d-pc. Never renumber;
	  append. 0 (the default) = this defconfig declares no pairing and the
	  SPL check prints that it skipped -- set it in every *_libre board
	  config that ships an scp slice.

config MESON_GX_LIBRE_SECURE_LAYOUT
	bool "Open TF-A secure world in the first 16 MiB of DRAM"
	depends on MESON_GX
	help
	  Say Y on a board that boots OUR open TF-A, whose BL31 lives at
	  0x00100000 and whose SMC mailboxes live at 0x00200000 -- i.e. the
	  16 MiB block at DRAM start, with an optional OP-TEE band at its top:

	    0x00000000 +  1 MiB  NOT MEMORY     read-only ROM mirror (hwrom@0)
	    0x00100000 +  1 MiB  S-only, EL3    BL31 768K + SCP staging 256K
	    0x00200000 +  1 MiB  S<->NS shared  SMC shared mem + storage mailboxes
	    0x00300000 +  1 MiB  NS reserved    ramoops
	    0x00400000 + 12 MiB  OP-TEE band    when OP-TEE runs, else Linux
	    0x01000000 ->        NS free        Linux

	  This is a compile-time symbol rather than a device-tree property
	  because the LIBRE and the VENDOR build of a board share one board DTS
	  and one kernel DTB: libretech-cc_v2_defconfig takes the vendor
	  bl31.img, which loads at 0x05100000, while libretech-cc_v2_libre_-
	  defconfig runs the open TF-A at 0x00100000.  The DTB cannot tell which
	  firmware is flashed, so the FIRMWARE DESCRIBES ITSELF: U-Boot patches
	  the blob it hands Linux in ft_board_setup(), which runs on whatever
	  DTB is actually being booted (PXE-supplied, kernel-package or
	  built-in), not merely on U-Boot's own control FDT.

	  Say Y and this build will, in meson_init_reserved_memory():
	    - add /reserved-memory nodes for BL31 and the mailbox MiB (no-map)
	      and ramoops (mapped, compatible "ramoops"), keeping the SoC's own
	      hwrom@0,
	    - drop secmon@5000000, because BL31 is not there in this build,
	    - add the OP-TEE band and /firmware/optee only when OP-TEE answers
	      OPTEE_SMC_CALLS_UID, so one DTB serves both firmware builds.
	  The -libre board DTS already carries the always-present nodes; this
	  is defence in depth for a foreign DTB.  It also moves BL33 and the
	  FIT's BL31 load address out of the block -- see CONFIG_TEXT_BASE in
	  the *_libre defconfigs and the atf node in
	  arch/arm/dts/meson-gxl-u-boot.dtsi.
	  Design: amlogic/software/boot/design/libre-secure-block-and-optee.md.

	  Say N (the default) for the shipping vendor-firmware build, which is
	  left byte-identical.

	  Design: amlogic/software/boot/design/dram-reservations.md sections 9,
	  10a and 10b (D1).

config MESON_GX_LIBRE_SCMI_FIXUP
	bool "Hand Linux the open SC as SCMI: enable the DT carrier at boot"
	depends on MESON_GX && SCMI_FIRMWARE
	select OF_BOARD_FIXUP
	default n
	help
	  Say Y on a board whose open SC (SCP-firmware product amlogic/gxl)
	  answers SCMI on MHU channel 2 / cpu_scp_hpri -- the same SC this
	  U-Boot's own SCMI agent talks to.  The vendor build of the SAME
	  board runs the vendor BL30, which speaks SCPI on that mailbox, and
	  both builds boot the SAME kernel DTB.  So the DTB cannot say which
	  protocol the mailbox carries: the FIRMWARE DESCRIBES ITSELF, exactly
	  as MESON_GX_LIBRE_SECURE_LAYOUT does for the memory map.

	  The shipping kernel DTS carries the SCMI package DISABLED --
	  /firmware/scmi with protocol@11/@14/@12/@13 in that order, and the
	  always-on watchdog node -- and leaves /scpi enabled for the vendor
	  firmware.  Say Y and ft_board_setup() flips it on the blob it hands
	  Linux (PXE-supplied, kernel-package or built-in, not merely U-Boot's
	  control FDT).

	  It ASKS THE SC FIRST.  Enabling /firmware/scmi is an assertion about
	  hardware, so ft_board_setup() earns it: it sends one SCMI BASE
	  PROTOCOL_VERSION on the non-secure OSPM channel -- the same protocol,
	  channel and first message Linux's scmi_base_protocol_init() sends --
	  and requires a SUCCESS reply composed by the SC on this boot.  The AO
	  ready bits are checked first, but only as a safety interlock: they
	  survive a warm reset, so they can answer for the previous boot, and a
	  write into a window the SC has not opened raises an SError that resets
	  the board.  Bounded at 85 ms worst case, and 0 ms when no SC ran.

	  SC-DEPENDENT flips, made only when the SC answered:
	    - /firmware/scmi                 status -> "okay"
	    - /cpus/cpu@N  clocks            -> <&scmi protocol@14 0>
	                   power-domains     -> <&scmi protocol@13 0>
	                   power-domain-names = "perf"
	  These are load-bearing together (measured on AML-S905X-CC-V2): the
	  clock repoint is what lets the OPP core resolve a usable clock.  When
	  the SC does NOT answer, one line says so and both are left as the DTB
	  had them -- scmi disabled, no repoint -- so cpufreq simply does not
	  come up.  Without that guard, a build whose chain never starts an SC
	  still told Linux the mailbox was live, and scmi_probe() took a
	  synchronous external abort in shmem_tx_prepare() and never reached
	  userspace (measured 2026-08-30 on v2026.07/lc-master, which has the
	  fixup and not the DRAM SPL's SC loader).

	  SC-INDEPENDENT flips, made either way:
	    - /scpi                          status -> "disabled"
	      (it describes the VENDOR BL30's SCPI service on this mailbox, and
	      a -libre image never loads a vendor BL30, so the node is false on
	      this build whatever the SC did.  Left enabled with no SC at all,
	      Linux's SCPI driver reaches the same closed shared-memory window
	      the SCMI one does and takes the same synchronous external abort,
	      in scpi_process_cmd() -- measured 2026-08-30.  Guarding only the
	      SCMI half moves the fault, it does not remove it)
	    - amlogic,meson-gxl-ao-wdt       status -> "okay"
	      (the ROM arms this watchdog before it loads anything and
	      MESON_GXL_AO_WDT_HANDOFF refreshes it while U-Boot runs and, by
	      default, stops it before the OS; wdt_handoff=1 hands it over
	      running, for a kernel known to carry the driver.  The node is
	      enabled either way and involves no SC)
	  Two more, optional (a blob without the nodes is still whole), retire
	  descriptions of what the VENDOR BL31 offers and this one does not --
	  which BL31 is running is settled by the image, not by the SC:
	    - amlogic,meson-gx-pm            status -> "disabled"
	      (its suspend_ops out-rank psci's and turn `echo mem` into a raw
	      CPU_SUSPEND(0x0010000) whose refusal leaks to userspace as
	      ENOENT; measured 2026-08-29)
	    - arm,idle-state                 status -> "disabled"
	      (CPU_SLEEP_0 is a POWERDOWN idle with no wake path on the open
	      SC; refused, Linux retries it every ~18 us and never idles in
	      wfi; measured 2026-08-29)

	  It only FLIPS.  No node is created, no phandle is generated, nothing
	  is deleted: a blob that does not carry the disabled package (an old
	  DTB) is left untouched, one line names what is missing, and the boot
	  continues.  Every flip is printed so the console is the evidence.

	  IT ALSO GUARDS U-BOOT'S OWN CONTROL FDT, which is why it selects
	  OF_BOARD_FIXUP.  This U-Boot has an SCMI agent of its own, and the
	  /firmware/scmi node that binds it makes the same assertion about the
	  same hardware two stages before ft_board_setup() runs.  With no SC the
	  agent does not merely fail: UCLASS_SCMI_AGENT probes the base protocol
	  at DM BIND time and scmi_dt_get_smt_buffer() reads the shared-memory
	  window before it has sent anything, which on a window the SC never
	  opened is a synchronous external abort at 0xC8013410 and a reset
	  (measured 2026-08-30, N57).  So board_fix_fdt() asks the same question
	  in board_init_f -- one query per boot, cached, shared with the flips
	  above -- and on a mute SC REMOVES /firmware/scmi from U-Boot's own
	  control FDT, so the agent is never bound.  With a live SC it changes
	  nothing.  The node is deleted rather than disabled because the control
	  DTB has no slack to grow "okay" into "disabled", and because there is
	  no point in the agent's path at which it could decline instead.

	  This is deliberately a separate symbol from SCMI_FIRMWARE: that one
	  says U-Boot HAS an SCMI agent, which is also true on boards where
	  Linux must keep talking SCPI to a vendor SC.  Say N (the default)
	  for the shipping vendor-firmware build, which is left byte-identical.

	  Design: amlogic/software/boot/gxl/design/open-sc-architecture.md
	  sections 3 and 4.

config MESON_GX_SCMI_AP_AGENT
	bool "Bind an SCMI agent in U-Boot proper when the open SC is live"
	depends on MESON_GX_LIBRE_SCMI_FIXUP
	default n
	help
	  Say Y on the open-chain boot shell to make U-Boot's OWN control-FDT
	  /firmware/scmi node "okay" so DM binds the SCMI agent and the `scmi`
	  command can drive the open SC from the "=> " prompt (perf subscribe
	  + trigger, for the SCP->AP notification proof).

	  Boot is unchanged on every other build: this is default N, the node
	  stays "disabled", and no shipping DTB grows.  On a target that says Y
	  but boots a mute SC the MESON_GX_LIBRE_SCMI_FIXUP self-guard still
	  deletes the node in board_init_f before DM scans it, so the agent is
	  never bound and the SMT-window read that would abort never runs.  It
	  is a diagnostic enabler, not a boot path.

config MESON_GXL_AO_WDT_HANDOFF
	bool "Refresh the ROM's AO boot watchdog and hand it to the OS"
	depends on MESON_GXL && WDT
	default n
	help
	  The GXL BootROM arms the always-on watchdog at 0xC8100120 with a
	  5000 ms budget before it loads anything, and hands the board over
	  with it counting.  Nothing in the chain drives it: the only watchdog
	  DT node describes the EE/CBUS block at 0xC11098D0, a different device
	  that the ROM leaves stopped.  Measured on AML-S905X-CC-V2 with the
	  open chain: the kernel is entered at ~3.95 s of that budget, so an OS
	  whose watchdog driver probes at normal device-init time is reset
	  mid-init -- SMP bring-up alone reached 4.43 s.

	  Say Y and this build will
	    - pet the AO watchdog once its board init runs, and register a
	      cyclic pet so U-Boot's own duration stops mattering, and
	    - at the platform's last step before the OS (wdt_handoff_to_os(),
	      which needs CONFIG_WDT), STOP it by default (clear the enable
	      bit in the control register, GXBB_WDT_CTRL_EN = BIT(18)), so a
	      kernel without the watchdog driver is not reset 5 s in; or, with
	      the environment variable wdt_handoff=1, pet it a final time and
	      hand it over running with the FULL budget.

	  U-Boot refreshes the ROM watchdog while it runs and, by default,
	  stops it before the OS.  wdt_handoff=1 is for a kernel known to
	  carry the driver: it must then take ownership (Linux: a DT node for
	  the AO block plus CONFIG_WATCHDOG_HANDLE_BOOT_ENABLED) or it will
	  still be reset 5 s later, and for the right reason.  The refresh does
	  not extend the ROM's 5000 ms period.

	  Say N (the default) for the shipping vendor-firmware build, whose
	  earlier boot stages handle the block themselves.

	  Note for anyone extending this: the AO reset register at 0xC810012C
	  must never be READ -- a read stalls both masters on GXL.  Write only.

config MESON_G12A_LIBRE_SCMI_FIXUP
	bool "Hand Linux the SM1 open SC as SCMI: enable the DT carrier at boot"
	depends on MESON_G12A && SCMI_FIRMWARE
	select OF_BOARD_FIXUP
	default n
	help
	  The SM1 twin of MESON_GX_LIBRE_SCMI_FIXUP, same contract.  The -libre
	  DTB carries the open SC's SCMI package DISABLED
	  (meson-sm1-libre-scmi.dtsi: /firmware/scmi with protocol@11 @14 @13
	  @15, the MHU at 0xFF63C404, one HP channel at 0xFFFE7600) and CPUs
	  with no OPP table.  ft_board_setup() first checks the SC's ready
	  paint (AO_RTI_SCP_STAT[21:20], a safety interlock only, because it
	  survives a warm reset), then sends ONE SCMI BASE PROTOCOL_VERSION on
	  the NS HP channel.  Only a SUCCESS reply composed by the SC on this
	  boot earns the flips, on the blob handed to Linux:
	    - /firmware/scmi and /soc/mailbox@ff63c404  status -> "okay"
	    - /cpus/cpu@N  clocks -> <protocol@14 0>, power-domains ->
	      <protocol@13 0>, power-domain-names = "perf"
	  Otherwise one line names the reason and the blob is untouched, so
	  Linux runs with no cpufreq at the clock the SPL left.  A blob without
	  the package (the vendor-leaf DTB) is left as it is.  Nothing is
	  created and no phandle is minted.

	  It also guards U-Boot's OWN control FDT from board_fix_fdt(): with no
	  SC it deletes /firmware/scmi before DM binds U-Boot's SCMI agent.
	  One query per boot, cached and shared by both call sites.

	  Say N (the default) for every build whose BL30 is not the open SC: a
	  BASE probe into a vendor SCPI BL30 is an untested message to foreign
	  firmware.

	  Design: amlogic/software/boot/g12/sm1-linux-scmi-design.md section 3.

config MESON_G12A_LIBRE_SECURE_LAYOUT
	bool "Open TF-A secure world in the first 16 MiB of DRAM (G12A/G12B/SM1)"
	depends on MESON_G12A
	help
	  Say Y on a G12A / G12B / SM1 board that boots OUR open TF-A, whose
	  BL31 lives at 0x00100000 and whose SMC mailboxes live at 0x00200000:

	    0x00000000 +  1 MiB  NS reserved    empty (see below)
	    0x00100000 +  1 MiB  S-only, EL3    BL31
	    0x00200000 +  1 MiB  S<->NS shared  SMC shared mem + storage mailboxes
	    0x00300000 +  1 MiB  NS reserved    ramoops
	    0x00400000 + 12 MiB  OP-TEE band    when OP-TEE runs, else Linux
	    0x01000000 ->        NS free        BL33, then Linux

	  This is the GXL block of CONFIG_MESON_GX_LIBRE_SECURE_LAYOUT exactly
	  (operator decisions 2026-09-26 and 2026-10-17).  On GXL the first megabyte
	  is a read-only ROM mirror (hwrom@0) that neither EL3 nor Linux can
	  use, so its reservation costs nothing.  G12A/G12B/SM1 have no mirror
	  -- the low megabyte is ordinary, allocator-owned DRAM, measured 2026-
	  08-21 on A311D and S905D3 -- so reserving it costs one megabyte per
	  board.  That price buys back decision A5's property, which the brief
	  bottom-megabyte divergence had given up: ramoops has ONE address,
	  0x00300000, across all families, and no pstore-aware tool needs a
	  family test.

	  Like the GXL symbol this is compile-time rather than a device-tree
	  property, because the LIBRE and the VENDOR build of a board share one
	  board DTS and one kernel DTB: aml-a311d-cc_defconfig takes the vendor
	  bl31.img, which loads at 0x05100000, while the -libre build runs the
	  open TF-A at 0x00100000.  The DTB cannot tell which firmware is
	  flashed, so the FIRMWARE DESCRIBES ITSELF -- U-Boot patches the blob
	  it hands Linux in ft_board_setup(), which runs on whatever DTB is
	  actually being booted, not merely on U-Boot's own control FDT.

	  Say Y and this build will, in meson_init_reserved_memory():
	    - add /reserved-memory nodes for ramoops (MAPPED, compatible
	      "ramoops"), the 2 MiB secure band and the mailbox MiB (both
	      no-map),
	    - drop secmon@5000000 and secmon@5300000, because BL31 and a BL32
	      are not there in this build,
	    - drop any OTHER ramoops node, because pstore binds exactly one,
	    - add the OP-TEE band and /firmware/optee only when OP-TEE answers
	      OPTEE_SMC_CALLS_UID.
	  It also moves the FIT's BL31 load address out of 0x05100000 -- see
	  the atf node in arch/arm/dts/meson-g12-common-u-boot.dtsi.

	  BL33 does NOT move on this family.  CONFIG_TEXT_BASE stays
	  0x01000000, which is now the first byte ABOVE the block -- the same
	  geometry GXL has, except GXL's vendor BL33 had to be moved there
	  while this family's already sat at it.  The OP-TEE band now abuts
	  BL33 with zero clearance: growing OP-TEE past 12 MiB would grow the
	  block into BL33, and is a constants-and-rebuild change, not a
	  one-property edit.

	  Say N (the default) for the shipping vendor-firmware build, which is
	  left byte-identical.

	  Design: amlogic/software/boot/design/libre-secure-block-and-optee.md;
	  history in dram-reservations.md sections 9, 10a, 10b (A4/A5) and 10i.

