config MESON_TIMER_RATE_FIXUP
	bool "Publish a known CNTPCT_EL0 rate to get_tbclk()"
	depends on ARM64 && SYS_ARCH_TIMER
	default y if MESON_GXL
	select ARMV8_CNTFRQ_BROKEN
	help
	  udelay() computes its target as usec * get_tbclk() / 1000000, and
	  get_tbclk() reads CNTFRQ_EL0.  On this SPL that register reads ZERO:
	  CONFIG_COUNTER_FREQUENCY is 0, and the only msr that would consume it
	  (arch/arm/cpu/armv8/start.S) sits behind branch_if_not_highest_el
	  while this stage runs at EL1, where CNTFRQ_EL0 is read-only.  With a
	  zero rate the target is start + 1 tick, so the first get_ticks()
	  comparison already passes and EVERY udelay() returns after the cost
	  of two counter reads -- measured on AML-S905X-CC as ~4.09 us for
	  udelay(1), udelay(100) and udelay(1000) alike.  Every settle in DRAM
	  bring-up collapses to that one value.

	  Say Y to measure the counter against ISA_TIMERE (the BootROM's 1 MHz
	  counter) over 10 ms at the top of board_init_f(), before any udelay()
	  can run, write the result into gd->arch.timer_rate_hz, and select
	  ARMV8_CNTFRQ_BROKEN so get_tbclk() prefers it.  The rate is not one
	  constant: the dram-bench stage measured 333.33 MHz on AML-S905X-CC,
	  and the DRAM SPL counts 600 MHz on AML-S805X-AC-V2 and
	  AML-S905X-CC-V2.  MESON_TIMER_RATE_HZ is used only if ISA_TIMERE
	  does not advance.  The preference
	  is conditional on the field being non-zero, so a build that does not
	  set it keeps the old mrs behaviour exactly.

config MESON_TIMER_RATE_HZ
	int "CNTPCT_EL0 tick rate in Hz"
	depends on MESON_TIMER_RATE_FIXUP
	default 333333333 if MESON_GXL
	default 0
	help
	  The rate the generic counter actually ticks at, which is a property
	  of the SoC and NOT of anything this tree configures.

	  333333333 is the GXL figure and it is a MEASUREMENT, not a databook
	  value: the dram-bench clock stage counted 33333978 CNTPCT ticks
	  against 100002 ISA_TIMERE ticks on AML-S905X-CC, giving 333332563 Hz,
	  which is 1000/3 MHz to within 0.00007%.  Do not read that agreement
	  as more than it is -- the derivation assumes ISA_TIMERE's input
	  select 1 really is 1 us, and nothing in this tree proves the decade.
	  What it does exclude is 24000000, the value an arch timer is usually
	  given and which the dead benchmark used to assume: that is wrong here
	  by 13.9x.

	  Erring HIGH is the safe direction -- delays come out proportionally
	  long, and a gross overestimate trips the SPL's 8 s watchdog, which is
	  loud.  Erring low shortens every settle silently, which is the defect
	  this symbol exists to fix.  Leave it 0 to keep the old behaviour.

choice
	prompt "DRAM rank mode"
	default DRAM_ONE_RANK
	help
	  How many ranks the fitted part has.  This depends on the board; a
	  board config fragment should state it rather than leave it to the
	  default.

	  The default is deliberate and is the safe end of the choice, not a
	  guess: one rank programs one DMC range, so a two-rank board built
	  without a fragment loses its upper rank and reports the smaller size
	  correctly.  It can no longer fold the window -- the address map is
	  the same either way and it is sized to one rank, and dram_init()
	  narrows the range decode to whatever detection actually found.  The
	  wrong choice therefore costs capacity, never correctness.

