config MESON_DRAM_DETECT_REF_OFFSET
	hex "Size-detect reference cell, as an offset into DRAM"
	default 0x1000000
	help
	  Offset of the size detector's reference cell from CFG_SYS_SDRAM_BASE.
	  The default 16 MB is inside rank 0 for every size this DMC can be
	  programmed with, and -- crucially -- is at offset 0 within its MB,
	  while every marker the detector writes sits at 0xffffc within its own.
	  No power-of-2 fold can therefore map a marker onto the reference, so
	  under the default the reference cell is the one word in the detector
	  that only the detector itself writes.

	  Moving it ONTO a marker address is what makes the bail path reachable
	  on healthy silicon.  The marker loop then clobbers the reference with
	  `nonce ^ size_mb`, the reference check fails against a real readback
	  rather than an injected constant, and dram_init() refuses:

	    (128 << 20) - 4   the LAST marker written  -> why=3 (bus-echo arm)
	    any other marker                           -> why=1 (size/map arm)

	  This is a broken WINDOW, not a fault injection: every write, readback
	  and compare on the path is the shipping one.  It exists so N178's
	  "DDR: size detect REFUSED why=" can be observed on hardware, which no
	  healthy board can otherwise produce.  Do not ship a non-default value
	  -- a board that boots with one has no size detection at all.

config MESON_DRAM_PGSR0_TIMEOUT_US
	int "PGSR0 all-done wait, in microseconds (0 = the built-in 500000)"
	default 0
	help
	  Bound on the wait for the PHY to report every done bit in PGSR0.  The
	  built-in 500000 (0.5 s) is ~3 orders of magnitude over what this
	  hardware takes, so on a healthy board the wait never expires and
	  `PGSR0 timeout!` -- with the whole retry ladder behind it -- cannot be
	  observed.

	  Set it to something the PHY cannot possibly meet (100 works) and the
	  wait genuinely expires: the attempt fails with err=-1, the ladder
	  prints `DDR attempt n/3 failed`, re-enters from reset, and finally
	  gives up with `DDR init FAILED after 3 attempts`.  Every one of those
	  lines is shipping code taking a real timeout -- nothing is injected.

	  This is N27's residual (a): that failure path has never printed on a
	  production image.  Leave it at 0 for anything that boots a board for
	  real; a short bound means DRAM never trains.

config MESON_DRAM_PCTL_STATE_TIMEOUT_US
	int "PCTL state wait, in microseconds (0 = the built-in 100000)"
	default 0
	help
	  Bound shared by every PCTL state wait -- the GO->ACCESS transition in
	  meson_dram_finalise_init() and the bit-0 waits behind WAIT_FOR().

	  Shortened, it reaches the other two messages N27 left unexercised:
	  `PCTL ACCESS timeout! STAT=%08x` and
	  `DDR wait <who>: bit0 never set, val=%08x (raw)`.  Same reasoning and
	  same caveat as MESON_DRAM_PGSR0_TIMEOUT_US above: a compile-time
	  constant, inert at 0, and not something to ship.

config MESON_DRAM_PCTL_ACCESS_TIMEOUT_US
	int "PCTL GO->ACCESS wait, in microseconds (0 = follow the shared bound)"
	default 0
	help
	  The GO->ACCESS wait in meson_dram_finalise_init() only.  Inert at 0,
	  where it follows the shared bound exactly as before.

	  It exists because of two measurements, 2026-08-30, and it does NOT do
	  what it was added to do:

	  1. Shortening the shared MESON_DRAM_PCTL_STATE_TIMEOUT_US never
	     reaches this wait at all -- the bit-0 waits earlier in the sequence
	     take the same bound and fail first every time, so
	     `PCTL ACCESS timeout!` is MASKED by `bit0 never set`.  Separating
	     the bounds is necessary to reach it.
	  2. Necessary, but not sufficient: with this symbol at 100 AND at 1,
	     the board boots normally, 1024 MB, try 1.  The controller is
	     already in ACCESS on the FIRST read, so the loop never iterates and
	     no bound, however small, can make it expire.

	  So `PCTL ACCESS timeout!` is NOT provocable by timing on a healthy
	  board.  It is a real guard for a real wedge -- the one that held the
	  console dark before it was bounded -- and only a controller that
	  genuinely never reaches ACCESS will print it.  Do not read its silence
	  as the guard being untested; read it as the transition being fast.

config MESON_DRAM_DETECT_MIN_MB
	int "Smallest size-detect marker, in MB (0 = the built-in 128)"
	default 0
	help
	  The smallest marker the size detector writes, and so the LAST word its
	  marker loop stores.

	  Set ABOVE the detect maximum (rank0 + rank1) and both marker loops get
	  an empty range: nothing is written, nothing is read back, the
	  reference still holds the nonce, and no cell holds its own marker.
	  That is exactly the state why=2 names, and it is the only way to reach
	  it -- a misordered window is a real configuration fault, not an
	  injected value, and refusing is the correct response.

	  Leave at 0 for anything real.  Inert there.

config MESON_DRAM_FAIL_FIRST_ATTEMPTS
	int "Apply the short PGSR0 bound to only the first N attempts (0 = all)"
	default 0
	help
	  The recovery instrument.  With MESON_DRAM_PGSR0_TIMEOUT_US shortened,
	  a single constant bound fails EVERY attempt identically, so the ladder
	  can only ever reach the give-up line.  Set this to 1 and only attempt
	  1 gets the short bound; attempt 2 gets the built-in 0.5 s and
	  succeeds, which is the only way to print `try 2` on a production
	  image -- the recovery half of N27, previously seen only on the
	  instrument lineage.

	  At 0 the entire arm, including the attempt counter it reads, is
	  compiled out, so the shipping stage carries none of it.

config MESON_DRAM_PHY_RESET_ASSERT
	bool "Assert DMC_SOFT_RST before releasing it (GXL)"
	default y
	help
	  The GXL vendor BL2 asserts DMC_SOFT_RST/RST1 before releasing them, so
	  a retry re-enters DRAM init from reset rather than from the wreckage
	  of the attempt that just failed.  N27 restored that assert; whether it
	  is what makes the ladder RECOVER, as opposed to the bounded waits that
	  landed alongside it, was never separated.

	  Turning this off is that separation, and nothing else: with the
	  recovery instrument armed, does attempt 2 still come up?

	  MEASURED 2026-08-30: it does.  With the assert off, the board recovers
	  at `try 2` exactly as with it on, and a no-induced-failure control with
	  the assert off boots normally on try 1.  So the BOUNDED WAITS are what
	  recover; re-entry from reset is not load-bearing for a retry entered
	  after a timeout.  It says nothing about a retry entered from genuinely
	  wedged state, which this arm cannot construct.

	  Say y -- it matches the GXL vendor BL2.  n is a measurement arm, not a
	  configuration.

config MESON_DRAM_ZQ_REPORT
	bool "Print the PHY's ZQ calibration result after training"
	default n
	help
	  Reads back DDR0_PUB_ZQCR, ZQ0PR/ZQ0SR and ZQ1PR/ZQ1SR after training
	  and memtest, and prints the programmed value beside the calibrated
	  one.

	  Nothing in this tree has ever read the SR side, which is why the PHY
	  drive/ODT/ZQ settings are on record as never measured (N46).  The
	  programmed value alone cannot distinguish "calibration converged on
	  what we asked for" from "it railed" or "it was overridden" -- only the
	  result register separates those.

	  Costs one printf, so it is off by default and the shipping stage is
	  unchanged.  Turn it on for a measurement boot.

config MESON_DRAM_ZQ0PR_OVERRIDE
	hex "Override PUB_ZQ0PR (0 = the board's own value)"
	default 0x0
	help
	  Measurement arm for N46, not a configuration.  The first ZQ readback
	  found ZQ0 and ZQ1 programmed identically and reporting differently
	  (sr0 = 0x30, sr1 = 0), and no vendor authority we hold defines the
	  ZQnSR fields -- secure_apb.h and cmd_ddr_test.c both give the address
	  only -- so the result cannot be decoded into ohms.

	  What can be established without a decode is whether each SR RESPONDS
	  to its PR.  Changing ZQ0's program value and watching sr0 is the
	  positive control for that; it is the arm that says the readback is a
	  live calibration result rather than a constant.

	  Changes the PHY's drive/ODT impedance, so a wrong value can cost
	  training.  Leave at 0.

config MESON_DRAM_ZQ1PR_OVERRIDE
	hex "Override PUB_ZQ1PR (0 = the board's own value)"
	default 0x0
	help
	  The other half of the N46 arm above.  If sr1 stays 0 while ZQ1's
	  program value changes, ZQ1's calibration does not run on this board --
	  which would explain the asymmetry without any field decode at all.

	  Leave at 0.

config MESON_GXL_VDDEE_PWM_B
	hex "GXL VDDEE PWM_B duty word (0 = do not program VDDEE)"
	depends on MESON_GXL
	default 0x0
	help
	  {high, low} duty pair written to PWM_PWM_B by meson_power_init()
	  immediately before dram_init().  On boards where PWM_B carries
	  VDDEE, the DDR PHY's digital logic and its master delay lines run
	  on this rail, so leaving it at the PWM reset default calibrates the
	  PHY against the wrong reference.

	  This is a PER-BOARD value taken from that board's own vendor BL2
	  voltage table -- do not copy it between boards.  0 leaves the rail
	  alone, which is what the SPL did before this option existed.

config DRAM_DQS_CORR
	bool "Enable DQS correction"

config DRAM_CLK
	int "DRAM clock"
	default 912
	help
	  This option contains the DRAM clock to use in MHz.

config DRAM_CLK_FALLBACK1
	int "DDR3: first fallback DRAM clock (0 = none)"
	depends on MESON_GXL && DRAM_DDR3
	default 0
	help
	  The clock dram_init() retries at when an attempt at DRAM_CLK fails
	  training, detects less than it trained, or fails the memtest.  The
	  ladder puts memory before clock: every clock is tried with both
	  ranks before any is tried with one, and a board that ends below
	  DRAM_CLK, or below its two-rank size after rank 1 trained, boots
	  with a WARNING line.  Must be below DRAM_CLK, at least 800 (the
	  slowest sourced speed bin in dram-timings-gx.h) and a multiple of
	  24 (the DDR PLL step above 800 MHz).

config DRAM_CLK_FALLBACK2
	int "DDR3: second fallback DRAM clock (0 = none)"
	depends on MESON_GXL && DRAM_DDR3
	default 0
	help
	  The next rung below DRAM_CLK_FALLBACK1, with the same rules; only
	  used when DRAM_CLK_FALLBACK1 is set.

config DRAM_DDR3_N831_FLOORS
	bool "DDR3: floor tMOD, tZQCS and tZQoper at their datasheet minimums"
	depends on MESON_GXL && DRAM_DDR3
	default y
	help
	  The vendor's mod 12, zqcs 64 and zqcl 136 are short at 912 MHz
	  against tMOD max(12tCK, 15 ns), tZQCS max(64tCK, 80 ns) and tZQoper
	  max(256tCK, 320 ns); this programs 14 / 73 / 292 there, and the
	  clock-scaled values at other clocks.  Say n only to reproduce the
	  vendor literals.  Measured on one AML-S905X-CC unit at 912 MHz: 3 of
	  3 clean boots, generator bandwidth equal to the literals (gxl:N831,
	  round n869).

config DRAM_DDR3_ABOVE_2133
	bool "DDR3: allow 1067..1498 MHz with every time-bound field clock-scaled"
	depends on MESON_GXL && DRAM_DDR3
	help
	  Off by default.  Lifts the tFAW guard's 1066 MHz limit (DDR3-2133 is
	  the fastest DDR3 bin; no bin is defined above it) and holds each
	  time-bound field at no less than its DDR3-2133 minimum: tRP/tRCD
	  13.09 ns, tRAS 33 ns, tRC 46.09 ns, tRRD 6 ns, tMOD 15 ns, tZQCS
	  80 ns, tZQoper max(256 nCK, 320 ns), on top of the always-on floors
	  (tRTP/tWTR/tWR/tRFC/tXP/tCKE/tXPDLL).  CL stays 14 and CWL 10 (CL
	  15/16 are Reserved in the MR0 table) and the MR0 WR code clamps at
	  16, so both run below spec by construction.  An extrapolation, not
	  a qualified operating point: on one unit at room temperature 1080
	  to 1296 MHz passed (rounds n830, n833) and 1392 failed after
	  training (n833).

choice
	prompt "Enable DRAM 2T mode"
	default DRAM_1T_MODE
	help
	  Choose whenever to use 2T mode or not.

config DRAM_1T_MODE
	bool "Use DRAM 1T mode"

config DRAM_2T_MODE
	bool "Use DRAM 2T mode"

endchoice

